    <acqinfo id="aspace_6673521c40988d242be10dc3fc286bb8">
      <head>Acquisition Information</head>
      <p>John Wolcott first donated his materials to the M.E. Grenander Department of Special Collections and Archives in 2007, with additions in 2013. The records included in the 2007 donation related exclusively to Pinxter Fest, while the records included in the 2013 donation document Wolcott's work in historic preservation, archaeology, and environmental activism, particularly in regards to the Albany Pine Bush.</p>
    </acqinfo>
    <arrangement id="aspace_c09bf30492445ebefe2676475c7fa45f">
      <head>Arrangement of the Collection</head>
      <p>The collection is arranged chronologically. There are no series.</p>
    </arrangement>
    <bioghist id="aspace_73543c998e4819a723fe0fce12ced4a0">
      <head>Biographical Sketch</head>
      <p>John Wolcott was born on September 22, 1932, in the Albany, New York region. He was a lifelong community activist in the Capital Region, focusing on historic preservation and environmentalism. An article from the Albany <emph render="italic">Times Union</emph> describes him as "a passionate long-time defender of preserving the Pine Bush ecosystem, self-proclaimed Albany historian, and thorn in the side of developers and government officials for decades."</p>
      <p>Wolcott served as chairman of the Pinxter Committee of Albany, which coordinated the annual Pinxter Festival, also referred to as Pinkster, Dutch for Pentecost, or Whitsuntide. During the colonial period, Pinxter celebrations were held throughout eastern New York in communities of Dutch origins. Africans played a major role in organizing Pinxter, adding African music and dance to the festivities. In 1811 the Common Council of the City of Albany passed a Whitsuntide Holiday law which banned the essential elements of the festival and, as a result, the festival died out. In 1972, Pinxter was revived in Albany and celebrated on Sundays during Tulip Fest in Washington Park with craft vendors and musicians. It was often advertised as an illegal festival. Wolcott wrote several letters asking the 1811 law be repealed. The Whitsuntide Holiday Law was repealed in 2011, two hundred years after it was enacted.</p>
      <p>Wolcott was also involved in advocating for the preservation of the Albany Pine Bush beginning in the early 1970s. The Pine Bush is home to the largest inland pine barrens sand dunes in the United States, and to tens of thousands of species of flora and fauna, including the endangered Karner Blue Butterfly. Wolcott first became interested in the Pine Bush after visiting it in his early childhood. He was a founding member of the Pine Bush Ski Club, formed in 1976, and of Save the Pine Bush, Inc., established on February 6, 1978, to protest proposed development plans within the Pine Bush. Wolcott's involvement in Save the Pine Bush centered around conducting historical and archaeological research into the preserve, creating trail maps, and leading hikes and skiing trips through the Pine Bush. In 1999, Wolcott was involved in advocating for a hikers' underpass where Route 155 crosses over the New York State Thruway. In October 2025, the underpass was designated the John Wolcott Pine Bush Hikers' Underpass. </p>
      <p>Professionally, Wolcott was employed by the New York State Historic Trust as a member of the Fort Orange Excavation team from 1970-1971, and later by the Office of Real Property Tax Services, where he performed title searches into 17th, 18th, and 19th century deeds. He and his wife, Linda Becker, were married for nearly 50 years. He passed away on August 17, 2023, at the age of 90.</p>
      <p>For more information on Wolcott, see the <a href="https://savethepinebush.org/?s=john%20wolcott">website of Save the Pine Bush, Inc.</a></p>
    </bioghist>
